{:edb/db-before-t 989 :edb/db-after-t 990 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534302 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:47:03.009Z" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186161808 1071 "![](images/78/78735529c93fa6286edc0fdc69d7398ec9cf758f7e46a0fb71702300aadbeb19.png)\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$\n![](images/70/703a545742438a81183008b90c0336ee51e9fc230d72ed44b02dbd76f0292f7f.png)" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186161808 1071 "![](images/e5/e56fbd2ced76bc02d7c9b29a81f3afab000053d11ec878040f26cef6cc5f65ce.svg)\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$\n![](images/ba/ba1b9ecff7b4825d7eb4fa25ad706c14d04a82b8daa45e524d556c90d71700ca.svg)" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186161809 1071 "![](images/ea/ea3e5c13d236a733350a1d23b8629cd481f9316e16ab7558873e305fe198eb98.png)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186161809 1071 "![](images/f0/f0cb7d70ea0921d1e2e8f2f0a9d8bc06427f29e28b8ed6ea79b5db4ba132f1e0.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186161810 1071 "![](images/bb/bb8991a8e02d7280b3d24587067ebf0d7e5f5c0518a9f6b1d022e65ed1d42780.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186161810 1071 "![](images/cb/cb8af25c129aac1ef9268d108c073e4b895f5e154c26ddb5692fd527d4eaddc3.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186161810 1074 "If $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=25.$ Computing $A^T \\! A - 25 I,$ we get\n\n![](images/8c/8c5b88c719ee0e5495e9b0df2c9f4add97a668e74249680fbf378c7c775d92f2.svg)\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T\\!A-25I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/b3/b36812322e6f9128de625a8a2d4e7faf62ba98fb007224c819e2a05738cd7f98.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we get \n![](images/16/1608541af7edcbbb06504c97b907dc2c40a97d9a7c9575d5145c67785f9eb56c.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|v_{11}|+|v_{21}| = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| = \\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186161810 1074 "If $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=25.$ Computing $A^T \\! A - 25 I,$ we get\n\n![](images/be/be2d743322a536fc213e7e2a00a04c2b902ab15efafedd8a7e6f7935cd5c7592.png)\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T\\!A-25I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/1f/1f537b8ae115d1d01a03766d9c2006ca8bbee77e1b99228466642c5e89c0d0ef.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we get \n![](images/77/77be4b42f9c5a8b379de6a74088106b6374bf28c48e9a7ceb2beb55d483afafe.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|v_{11}|+|v_{21}| = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| = \\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186161811 1071 "![](images/4d/4d6b11d3095c2f0d39493b5ff95c16b3f7581938ced3e3eb6daa4daeacf74d55.png)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186161811 1071 "![](images/eb/eb6e660e7dd1c933fd2d5c6b6a05fbdf1fe7f5f8ed24faf15f88ea16d7d04889.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186161811 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-72 & -24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/29/296fe06c266dbb9b46bcd640204527e906be5d8a1c25aa371ff042b72e070eeb.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/70/7016fb0fa52eac439210abd557ae2f7ec79ab18b1902e2a65c98f4cf9217ae7e.png)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ed/ed1efc97e5dde1f464ad1bce0689dce9657449a7da56800f34f4429e4a7948df.png)" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186161811 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-72 & -24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/74/748df21fe8314e02f5a5253edaa39427c38600cd60099ccdfed075a28499d23e.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/08/08573d10464e356dc2eeb7a3a0a4c5f9255d0f96b14da9d63b718b766c1cc8d8.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/252867b0bccd95be918f05320ee65c7153f499b15ccc77624be18128d0f0de09.svg)" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418155 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418156 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418159 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418153 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418157 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418158 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418152 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418154 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162226 1050 1200.0 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161258 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186162682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161384 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186162682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418148 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162682 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162682 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$, $Σ$, and $V$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, what is the second column of $U$?" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186162682 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=8.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{32}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161420 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186162688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418147 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162688 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186162688 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V$?" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186162688 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}36 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 61\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}36 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 61\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-45 & 30 \\\\ 30 & -20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-81I)x=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}36 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 61\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}20 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)x=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186163232 1064 "The **singular value decomposition** (or SVD) of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is a factorization of the form\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m \\times m$ matrix, and\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n \\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe columns of $U$ are called the **left singular vectors** of $A,$ and the columns of $V$ are called the **right singular vectors** of $A.$\n\nFor example, the matrix ![](images/46/46b29ceb84e2ed98e48642e67277c9bf63d0552bcd07f813a9ffcaf665ff91b7.svg) has the following singular value decomposition:\n\n![](images/29/298933eb0a789ebe57a296088172f6927d1573770824c0b0cffb393eb9894c70.svg)\n\nThe singular value decomposition of a matrix $A$ can be thought of as a type of diagonalization that works for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix! It's also one of linear algebra's most useful forms of matrix factorization with applications in statistics, image compression, machine learning, and more." 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186163232 1064 "The **singular value decomposition** (or SVD) of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is a factorization of the form\n\n$$A=U \\Sigma V^T,$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m \\times m$ matrix, and\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n \\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe columns of $U$ are called the **left singular vectors** of $A,$ and the columns of $V$ are called the **right singular vectors** of $A.$\n\nFor example, the matrix $$A =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$$ has the following singular value decomposition:\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}_{A} = \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{U} \\: \\underbrace{\\vphantom{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{\\Sigma} \\: \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]^T}_{V^T}$$\n\nThe singular value decomposition of a matrix $A$ can be thought of as a type of diagonalization that works for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix! It's also one of linear algebra's most useful forms of matrix factorization with applications in statistics, image compression, machine learning, and more." 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186163246 1064 "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A.$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V.$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U.$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ let's find the matrices $U$ and $V.$\n\n![](images/ab/abe87fb2a304ff102bb3f703d025c7d41f56cb6fab7fc477b3073cbd820cda6e.svg)\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & =[\\begin{aligned}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/41/4195807c3f61cd730c57340666c8d651e94d2b277f9623da7cd482c1d7123303.svg)\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/64/64aa31f039ec188355522cffa8a6c62df8c53bb409e55df32ce880d9a9efcd6e.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab0a5a5a284c962414158e5b7c463264b271bdd57decd8e08305285f7243f7bd.svg)" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186163246 1064 "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A.$\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma.$\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V.$\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula $$\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,$$ where $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U.$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ let's find the matrices $U$ and $V.$\n\n$$A =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}], \\quad \\Sigma = [\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & =[\\begin{aligned}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9.$\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n$$\\mathbf{v}_1 = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].$$\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n$$\\mathbf{v}_2 = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].$$\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$U = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}], \\qquad V = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186163247 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and\n\n$$A= U\\Sigma V^T$$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition.\n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\ & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\ & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\ & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\ & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$A^TA = V\\left( \\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A.$ So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A.$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A,$ we obtain $$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$\n- Solving the characteristic equation $|A^T \\! A - \\lambda I| = 0,$ we get the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A \\mathbin{:}$ $${\\color{blue}{\\lambda_1 = 16}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2 = 1}}$$ So, the corresponding singular values are $${\\color{blue}\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} = 4}, \\qquad {\\color{red}\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = 1}.$$\n\nWe now find a unit eigenvector basis of $A^T \\! A.$ These unit eigenvectors will be the columns of our matrix $V$ in the orthogonal diagonalization of $A^T \\! A$.\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{blue}\\lambda_1} I),$ we obtain $$A^T \\! A - 16I = [\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}] - 16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}-12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].$$ Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get $${\\color{blue}{ [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}}.$$\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{red}\\lambda_2} I),$ we obtain $$A^T \\! A - I = [\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}] - [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].$$ Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get $${\\color{red} [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}.$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\Sigma = [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], \\qquad V = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 false] [17592186163247 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA= U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition. \n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$\nA^TA = V\\left( \\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A.$ So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A.$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/b7/b766ec7d8c00dfc121e707566d21db470c087c3e785f4e1b2868e7f932873c67.svg)\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 