ingular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418154 1072 #uuid "345be953-89e4-5540-a9e1-d54a38da924f" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418154 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418150 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418155 1069 "q-95901" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418155 1071 "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Σ$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- $U$ is a diagonal matrix" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418155 1072 #uuid "5d3ee109-94d4-53ed-8971-ac2ef97dce27" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418155 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $3\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix,the matrix $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $U$ must be orthogonal but not necessarily diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418144 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418156 1069 "q-96233" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418156 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418156 1072 #uuid "70b4f245-e7d8-5908-9e68-f829b0901af8" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418156 1074 "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418145 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418157 1069 "q-96305" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418157 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418157 1072 #uuid "9020d5f8-c5f3-54dc-9474-2d2b14ec7c2a" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418157 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418143 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418158 1069 "q-96309" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418158 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418158 1072 #uuid "b33c0902-677a-58a2-8ff6-887b643a37e9" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418158 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418142 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418159 1069 "q-95902" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418159 1071 "$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418159 1072 #uuid "d974fc39-79e3-5801-98d4-4a560ca244ac" 13194139534302 17592186045417 true] [17592186418159 1074 "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139534302 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0b06010d-965b-582a-8b2a-49ead930bd31" 17592186418092 "answer-0f85dcb3-9485-53ae-ae8a-22f225ec6ba6" 17592186418093 "answer-1445126a-2e39-5f06-b93c-d37d24ee2f19" 17592186418094 "answer-18398a20-b6f3-582f-801a-647783315331" 17592186418095 "answer-25daf057-15c3-5df1-bf49-09f15c637373" 17592186418096 "answer-3a7a632d-1cf9-5bef-a9fc-f46a7da7ca7f" 17592186418097 "answer-3ba44f9d-452d-5c1b-a60d-1fa5aefc80dd" 17592186418098 "answer-3eb57cdc-06ed-5be6-a085-358bde4fb1c4" 17592186418099 "answer-424fa9e5-1ef1-53f1-9fa6-4a3a184c8a15" 17592186418100 "answer-497e502c-a710-5896-85f2-7c55ee60c5a2" 17592186418101 "answer-53b67272-97c4-5cf9-9f1e-56b3cc74ec71" 17592186418102 "answer-55eda9fc-807f-5348-8813-d854a5b33209" 17592186418103 "answer-56b85f54-1a37-5dc2-9478-38992ce73b70" 17592186418104 "answer-5afeb808-2408-5544-91eb-6e8f26969b1c" 17592186418105 "answer-5bb86930-f90d-5b12-90e9-e48a9d51a232" 17592186418106 "answer-61832b4e-5333-5c0b-8d40-35e8f54352c2" 17592186418107 "answer-6a0c4372-f45d-517d-808e-21d13a9bf422" 17592186418108 "answer-6fb3ccbb-6f9a-5137-a3b4-d0a87f0e7267" 17592186418109 "answer-766d0c5a-5abc-5b7d-9ced-05213b2666d0" 17592186418110 "answer-7718b520-dda1-5f3a-bc7b-0d6b3cb9898b" 17592186418111 "answer-780c2e8d-9e3d-5f3e-8162-9411863f1b43" 17592186418112 "answer-781d2783-b360-5c7a-a6be-21fa965bb5ee" 17592186418113 "answer-79a6a184-7306-52e1-95cf-639b6b7e6197" 17592186418114 "answer-7c9664d0-c906-570b-a107-ae60f617c808" 17592186418115 "answ